#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4014376-4dae-56f1-b981-b35d3afab874" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Such that the Limit of a Piecewise Function Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1dbf0e-4825-58b0-a33c-3c172a949d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f1dbf0e-4825-58b0-a33c-3c172a949d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338505} :answer-field/id #uuid "a83994ea-97d2-5f3f-85cd-bbdebe4d60db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe5f2d43-9b41-5a57-a075-65354d6721c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167689" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists.\n\nIn order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left- and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n* Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}+{1}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =e+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+a.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+a) \\\\  & =1+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 1+a & =e+2 \\\\ a & =e+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a649e49-73c7-5470-8361-2002da74f234" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e18e3cc4-44e0-5469-98af-4c0bc11ea45c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{e+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e18e3cc4-44e0-5469-98af-4c0bc11ea45c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{e+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338508} :answer-field/id #uuid "84ed7191-fa05-54cc-8438-8151abad81cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a06ae8a3-e6d2-5ca0-919e-a29d0ee59dfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-167691" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=e+2\\ne 0.$\n\nApplying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}{x}^{3}+2}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2}{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{e+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3eaedf5-3c82-53ad-90b7-835cbf9bd510" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Interval Over Which the Average Rate of Change of a Function is a Given Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41688bf0-946a-5d63-9119-67a3d42b7613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41688bf0-946a-5d63-9119-67a3d42b7613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338511} :answer-field/id #uuid "42069d56-c0d8-5410-851c-23ec50d8c54f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a7342b9-5d95-5d61-95de-165eeb082b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167692" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Over the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nLet's calculate the average rate of change of $f(x)$ over the interval $[3,c]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(c)-f(3)}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{({c}^{2}-2c)-({3}^{2}-2\\cdot 3)}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}^{2}-2c-3}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(c-3)(c+1)}{c-3} \\\\  & =c+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want this average rate of change to be equal to $6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =6 \\\\ c+1 & =6 \\\\ c & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44f09c22-7107-5c80-9c91-a7b58cb639b2" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45abf224-ee7d-5977-bf26-2ceff8a92b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45abf224-ee7d-5977-bf26-2ceff8a92b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338514} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b3b1f2-c645-566c-9ea8-0fe6a6995e68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0ca7998-3cbb-5b20-b116-0d2939a37839" :question/math-academy-id "q-167693" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=0.$ The step size, in this case, is $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & ≈\\frac{f(0+1)-f(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)-f(0)}{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{1}+{1}^{2}+1-({e}^{0}+{0}^{2}+0) \\\\  & =e+2-1 \\\\  & =e+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7199d82-cc45-5c65-892c-7566591bb03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^x+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7199d82-cc45-5c65-892c-7566591bb03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^x+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338517} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a700adb-7c92-5dab-9070-199150114f56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d61b841-ccdd-5f9c-a721-04a0fae8100f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167694" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "For $x<1,$ we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x,$ which is differentiable.\n\nUsing the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ffbc4bb-e179-543f-bcb5-3aaf6540a8a1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa107e5-66e5-53ea-9902-d870de89b8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aa107e5-66e5-53ea-9902-d870de89b8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338520} :answer-field/id #uuid "749670f8-50c8-5517-83b5-2ec9afd90050" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fac7bb0b-6f6f-5e64-b5f9-2e2593703827" :question/math-academy-id "q-167695" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "We use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x$ for $x\\le 1.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(0).$ In part 5, we found that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{x}+2x+1,\\,x<1.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}+2(0)+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db808f48-6d0c-5b35-9342-b1817df65172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cef946-43c0-5472-9cf1-e8b449ec6fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cef946-43c0-5472-9cf1-e8b449ec6fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338523} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8b10ee-5eec-565c-a361-86cc6e203b08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2095acb3-f5b6-5091-b016-7d439d771513" :question/math-academy-id "q-167696" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $A$ is $y=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "In part 6, we found that the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm=2.\n$$\n\nTo find the equation of the tangent, we use the point-slope form with the coordinates of $A$ and $m.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(1) & =(2)(x-0) \\\\ y-1 & =2x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c8ba29-95c9-5494-be0b-9b67bee6e45c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining where a Tangent Line Intersects an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88ddc722-ccc8-5ec3-9e7b-37766efec2b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88ddc722-ccc8-5ec3-9e7b-37766efec2b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338526} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6287a4c-a107-50e6-8df2-56fda468cc7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b12eb4e5-ce17-5a3d-b7f0-e21d815e9e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-167697" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $A$ is $y=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of $B,$ the point where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $A$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The $x$-coordinate of $B$ is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line is\n\n$$\ny=2x+1.\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intersects the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822e7603-642b-51af-b9dd-abf7c4dc7847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "822e7603-642b-51af-b9dd-abf7c4dc7847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338529} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e6514d6-689c-5bcc-bbd9-6068e36742ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67836926-88a9-55f9-9f2b-6910b74a7f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-167698" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $A$ is $y=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of $B,$ the point where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $A$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The $x$-coordinate of $B$ is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{1}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/b52bd85700ddc19132ab0cbd0c9243db92bb16145c16bf9bd5b0b0fa56434d9f.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\ge 3$ is shown above. Estimate the area $A$ under the curve over the interval $[4,7]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ rectangles of equal width.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x\\ge 3,$ we have that $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nFirst, let's compute the step size $Δx,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our integral are $a=4$ and $b=7,$ and there are $n=3$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{7-4}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/18d2d2d79746d5782d27769b644b31f36c76fc7cf9d867788490d74935439b4e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)+f(5)+f(6)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n| $5$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(8+15+24)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6d26561-ab7a-50c6-858f-33f211fc3cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d26561-ab7a-50c6-858f-33f211fc3cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338532} :answer-field/id #uuid "8debbda0-ab9b-5dec-b3f2-1270377cad1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338531} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1876c6b6-2344-5cdf-a287-21020ca49dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{n}\\left(\\left(4+\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)^2-2\\left(4+\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1876c6b6-2344-5cdf-a287-21020ca49dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{n}\\left(\\left(4+\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)^2-2\\left(4+\\frac{3k}{n}\\right)\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338534} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e280c85-d8e8-5dc7-9776-323f864a2a97" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb814d14-bc3a-58f3-a0ee-994c3b65af5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167699" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $A$ is $y=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of $B,$ the point where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $A$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The $x$-coordinate of $B$ is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{1}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 9:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/b52bd85700ddc19132ab0cbd0c9243db92bb16145c16bf9bd5b0b0fa56434d9f.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\ge 3$ is shown above. Estimate the area $A$ under the curve over the interval $[4,7]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ rectangles of equal width.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $47$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite down an expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral, and express the integral as the limit of a left Riemann sum. You do not need to simplify your answers.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A={\\int }_{4}^{7}$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ and the $x$-axis between $x=4$ and $x=7$ as the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{4}^{7}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n\t\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral above, the function is $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x,$ the lower bound is $a=4,$ and the upper bound is $b=7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7-4}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{7}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(4+\\frac{3k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3k}{n})}^{2}-2(4+\\frac{3k}{n})]\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$"}]}
